{
  "requirement": "A provider failure that ends the model conversation returns\n`ValidationError::Provider` with the underlying I/O error to the awaiting ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. When a provider failure ends the model conversation, the awaiting ask builder gets `ValidationError::Provider` carrying the underlying I/O error.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[assistant_text_and_provider_failure_cannot_become_typed_results]` covers this directly.\n  - It runs a `ToolAgent` over a `Script` provider with a single `None` turn.\n  - That scripted provider's `complete` returns `io::Error::other(\"permanent provider failure\")`.\n  - The test asserts that `ctx.ask::<bool>().await` returns `Err(ValidationError::Provider(error))`, and that `error.to_string()` contains \"permanent provider failure\". That string comes from the underlying I/O error, so the assertion checks that the I/O error is carried through.\n  - It also asserts that no dependencies were recorded.\n  - A wrong variant such as `Agent` or `Graph` would fail the test. So would a replaced or lost underlying error.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configured_agents_initialize_only_when_asked_and_report_setup_errors]` is a second test. A configured agent with a missing credential makes the awaited `verify` fail with `ValidationError::Provider`. The test also asserts that the message contains the credential variable name.\n- Both tests carry `{test}` markers for the subject, so there is no marking gap.\n\nI found no distinct failure mode that the text names but the tests omit. The requirement says nothing about retry exhaustion, and retry behavior is covered by other requirements.",
  "references": "0fbc2bd5fac34b56faf1a32f5f18e07e5f7165136fc32734f4466834b4898eda",
